From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 03 Nov 2026 03:19:03 +0000
Subject: media: cedrus: mpeg2: clamp motion compensation to the decoded frame

The boundary register (VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE) is where the engine
clamps the motion compensation reads from the references. It was set
from the OUTPUT format, the size userspace sets. FFmpeg sets the
display size, 1920x1080 for a 1080p or 1080i stream, so the engine
clamped the references to line 1079. The reference pictures of ISO/IEC
13818-2 are the whole decoded frame, rounded up to the macroblock rows
(6.3.3: mb_height, pairs of rows for interlaced sequences), and vectors
may point into the decoded lines 1080 to 1087. Those blocks were
predicted from the repeated line 1079 instead.

On an Orange Pi Zero2 (H616) a 1080p60 MP@HL stream (FFmpeg encoder,
40 Mbit/s) had errors of up to 21 in the bottom macroblock row,
spreading through the GOP; the same encode at 1920x1088 matched the
software decoder to IDCT rounding (max 3). The ISO 13818-4 main
profile streams are all 480 or 576 lines and did not show it.

Take the boundary from the sequence header: the width and the height
rounded up to the macroblock rows, halved for field pictures.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_mpeg2.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_mpeg2.c
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@
 static int cedrus_mpeg2_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, struct cedrus_run *run)
 {
 	struct vb2_buffer *fwd, *bwd;
+	unsigned int mb_height;
 	bool field, tff;
 	const struct v4l2_ctrl_mpeg2_sequence *seq;
 	const struct v4l2_ctrl_mpeg2_picture *pic;
@@ -142,9 +143,22 @@
 
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_DEC_MPEG_PICCODEDSIZE, reg);
 
-	reg = VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE_WIDTH(ctx->src_fmt.width);
-	reg |= VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE_HEIGHT(field ? ctx->src_fmt.height / 2 :
-						   ctx->src_fmt.height);
+	/*
+	 * Motion compensation clamps the references to the boundary. The
+	 * reference pictures are the whole decoded frame, which ISO/IEC
+	 * 13818-2 6.3.3 rounds up to the macroblock rows (pairs of them for
+	 * interlaced sequences), so vectors may point below a 1080 line
+	 * picture into the decoded rows 1080 to 1087. The OUTPUT format size
+	 * is what userspace set (FFmpeg uses the display size) and is not
+	 * that boundary.
+	 */
+	mb_height = seq->flags & V4L2_MPEG2_SEQ_FLAG_PROGRESSIVE ?
+		    DIV_ROUND_UP(seq->vertical_size, 16) :
+		    2 * DIV_ROUND_UP(seq->vertical_size, 32);
+
+	reg = VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE_WIDTH(ALIGN(seq->horizontal_size, 16));
+	reg |= VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE_HEIGHT(field ? mb_height * 8 :
+						   mb_height * 16);
 
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_DEC_MPEG_PICBOUNDSIZE, reg);
 
